=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The fault is not a physical WAN link failure as the ticket suspected. Router2's
eth2 (wan_r1_r2, IP 20.20.0.2/8) is fully UP and LOWER_UP at both L1 and L2,
and the WAN subnet is reachable. The actual broken component is the RIP routing
daemon (ripd) on router2, which was disabled during last night's maintenance.

In /etc/frr/daemons on router2, the line "ripd=no" explicitly prevents the FRR
init system from starting ripd. As a result, only zebra, mgmtd, and staticd are
running on router2 — no RIP process exists at all. The ripd.conf is also 0
bytes (empty), and no integrated frr.conf is present. Without ripd, router2
never exchanges RIP updates with router1 across the 20.20.0.x WAN link, so its
kernel and FRR routing tables contain only the three directly connected prefixes
(1.0.0.0/8, 128.168.0.0/16, 20.0.0.0/8). There are no routes to any of the
192.168.x.x department subnets served by router0 and router1.

When hosts in the Server Room (1.0.0.x) or Internet Lab (128.168.0.x) send
packets destined for 192.168.x.x, those packets arrive at router2 (their
default gateway), which immediately returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable"
— confirmed by live ping tests. The reverse path also fails: replies from
192.168.x.x hosts reach router2 but are silently dropped because no matching
route exists, causing timeouts.

Root cause: /etc/frr/daemons on router2 was changed to ripd=no (and ripd.conf
was cleared) during maintenance, disabling RIP and stranding router2's subnets
from the rest of the network.

Fix: On router2, edit /etc/frr/daemons and set ripd=yes, then populate
/etc/frr/ripd.conf with a "router rip" block advertising networks 1.0.0.0/8,
128.168.0.0/16, and 20.0.0.0/8 (matching the style used on router1), and
restart FRR ("systemctl restart frr"). RIP will reconverge with router1 and
full end-to-end connectivity will be restored.
=== END ===
```